search Assistant](https://bioengineer.org/category/bioengineering/careers/jobs/research-assistant/)\n- [EVENTS](https://bioengineer.org/category/explore/events/)\n- [iGEM](https://bioengineer.org/category/explore/igem/)\n- [News](https://bioengineer.org/category/explore/igem/news-igem/)\n- [Team](https://bioengineer.org/category/explore/igem/team/)\n- [PHOTOS](https://bioengineer.org/category/explore/photos/)\n- [VIDEO](https://bioengineer.org/category/explore/video-explore/)\n- [WIKI](https://bioengineer.org/category/explore/wiki/)\n- [BLOG](https://bioengineer.org/category/blog/)\n- [COMMUNITY](https://Bioengineer.org/)\n- [FACEBOOK](https://www.facebook.com/Bioengineer.org)\n- [INSTAGRAM](https://instagram.com/bioengineering/)\n- [TWITTER](https://twitter.com/bioengineerorg)\n\n- [HOME](https://bioengineer.org)\n- [NEWS](https://bioengineer.org/category/news/science-news/)\n- [EXPLORE](https://bioengineer.org/category/explore/)\n- [CAREER](https://bioengineer.org/category/bioengineering/careers/)\n- [Companies](https://bioengineer.org/category/bioengineering/careers/companies/)\n- [Jobs](https://bioengineer.org/category/bioengineering/careers/jobs/)\n- [Lecturer](https://bioengineer.org/category/bioengineering/careers/jobs/lecturer/)\n- [PhD Studentship](https://bioengineer.org/category/bioengineering/careers/jobs/phd-studentship/)\n- [Postdoc](https://bioengineer.org/category/bioengineering/careers/jobs/postdoc/)\n- [Research Assistant](https://bioengineer.org/category/bioengineering/careers/jobs/research-assistant/)\n- [EVENTS](https://bioengineer.org/category/explore/events/)\n- [iGEM](https://bioengineer.org/category/explore/igem/)\n- [News](https://bioengineer.org/category/explore/igem/news-igem/)\n- [Team](https://bioengineer.org/category/explore/igem/team/)\n- [PHOTOS](https://bioengineer.org/category/explore/photos/)\n- [VIDEO](https://bioengineer.org/category/explore/video-explore/)\n- [WIKI](https://bioengineer.org/category/explore/wiki/)\n- [BLOG](https://bioengineer.org/category/blog/)\n- [COMMUNITY](https://Bioengineer.org/)\n- [FACEBOOK](https://www.facebook.com/Bioengineer.org)\n- [INSTAGRAM](https://instagram.com/bioengineering/)\n- [TWITTER](https://twitter.com/bioengineerorg)\n\n[**](https://bioengineer.org/challenging-global-workspace-and-integrated-information-theories/)\n\nNo Result\n\n**View All Result\n\n[**](https://bioengineer.org/challenging-global-workspace-and-integrated-information-theories/)\n\n[](https://bioengineer.org/)\n\n[**](https://bioengineer.org/challenging-global-workspace-and-integrated-information-theories/)\n\nNo Result\n\n**View All Result\n\n[Home](https://bioengineer.org)**[NEWS](https://bioengineer.org/category/news/)**[Science News](https://bioengineer.org/category/news/science-news/)**[Health](https://bioengineer.org/category/news/science-news/health/)\n\n# Challenging Global Workspace and Integrated Information Theories\n\nby[Bioengineer](https://bioengineer.org/author/admin/)\n\n[April 30, 2025](https://bioengineer.org/challenging-global-workspace-and-integrated-information-theories/)\n\nin[Health](https://bioengineer.org/category/news/science-news/health/)\n\nReading Time: 5 mins read\n\n[**0](https://bioengineer.org/challenging-global-workspace-and-integrated-information-theories/#comments)\n\n[\n\n](https://bioengineer.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/Challenging-Global-Workspace-and-Integrated-Information-Theories.jpg)\n\n[**Share on Facebook](https://www.facebook.com/sharer.php?u=https%3A%2F%2Fbioengineer.org%2Fchallenging-global-workspace-and-integrated-information-theories%2F)[**Share on Twitter](https://twitter.com/intent/tweet?text=Challenging%20Global%20Workspace%20and%20Integrated%20Information%20Theories&url=https%3A%2F%2Fbioengineer.org%2Fchallenging-global-workspace-and-integrated-information-theories%2F)[**Share on Linkedin](https://www.linkedin.com/shareArticle?url=https%3A%2F%2Fbioengineer.org%2Fchallenging-global-workspace-and-integrated-information-theories%2F&title=Challenging%20Global%20Workspace%20and%20Integrated%20Information%20Theories)[**Share on Reddit](https://reddit.com/submit?url=https%3A%2F%2Fbioengineer.org%2Fchallenging-global-workspace-and-integrated-information-theories%2F&title=Challenging%20Global%20Workspace%20and%20Integrated%20Information%20Theories)[**Share on Telegram](https://telegram.me/share/url?url=https%3A%2F%2Fbioengineer.org%2Fchallenging-global-workspace-and-integrated-information-theories%2F&text=Challenging%20Global%20Workspace%20and%20Integrated%20Information%20Theories)[**](http://vk.com/share.php?url=https%3A%2F%2Fbioengineer.org%2Fchallenging-global-workspace-and-integrated-information-theories%2F)\n\n[**](https://www.linkedin.com/shareArticle?url=https%3A%2F%2Fbioengineer.org%2Fchallenging-global-workspace-and-integrated-information-theories%2F&title=Challenging%20Global%20Workspace%20and%20Integrated%20Information%20Theories)[**](https://www.pinterest.com/pin/create/bookmarklet/?pinFave=1&url=https%3A%2F%2Fbioengineer.org%2Fchallenging-global-workspace-and-integrated-information-theories%2F&media=https://bioengineer.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/Challenging-Global-Workspace-and-Integrated-Information-Theories.jpg&description=Challenging%20Global%20Workspace%20and%20Integrated%20Information%20Theories)[**](https://bioengineer.org/challenging-global-workspace-and-integrated-information-theories/)\n\nA ground-breaking adversarial collaboration has recently embarked on a bold mission to rigorously test and challenge two of neuroscience’s leading theories of consciousness: the Integrated Information Theory (IIT) and the Global Neuronal Workspace Theory (GNWT). By meticulously designing experiments that pit these contrasting frameworks against each other on common empirical grounds, the research team aims to transcend confirmation biases and break entrenched theoretical echo chambers that often impede progress in consciousness science.\n\nThis collaboration embraces a sophisticated falsificationist philosophy inspired by philosopher Imre Lakatos, framing theory testing not as a quest for outright confirmation but as a nuanced evaluation where failed predictions provide critical insights. The consortium’s multi-modal study combined intracranial EEG (iEEG), magnetoencephalography (MEG), and functional magnetic resonance imaging (fMRI) to overcome the limitations inherent in any single measurement technique, thereby producing some of the most robust data to date on the neural mechanisms underlying conscious experience.\n\nKey among the challenges to Integrated Information Theory was the absence of sustained synchronization within the posterior cortex—a fundamental prediction according to IIT, which claims that the state of a neural network’s activity and connectivity directly encodes conscious content and its degree. Despite the sophisticated multimodal design and ample statistical power, no long-lasting synchrony was observed in these posterior regions, raising urgent questions about the neural underpinnings IIT posits as essential to consciousness.\n\nIntriguingly, although IIT’s prediction concerning stimulus duration representation was met, the theory faltered on accounting for the sustained representation of stimulus orientation, an essential feature of the consciously perceived visual stimuli used in the study. Importantly, orientation information was successfully decoded across all three brain recording modalities, underscoring that the neural system does maintain such information, but IIT’s framework appears insufficient to explain how this perceptual feature persists in consciousness over time.\n\nTurning to the Global Neuronal Workspace Theory, this study confronted GNWT with a major unexpected result: the lack of “ignition” at stimulus offset. The global workspace model predicts that conscious perception is sustained by a cascade of widespread neuronal activity that should update with changes in conscious content—including the shift at the end of stimulus presentation. Yet, robust offset responses were missing from prefrontal cortex, despite strong onset responses to the very same stimuli. This leaves a striking gap in how GNWT accounts for the maintenance and updating of conscious percepts.\n\nThe results also challenge GNWT’s claim regarding the role of prefrontal cortex in broadcasting the full content of conscious experience. While category-level information was reliably decoded from prefrontal activity regardless of task demands, finer details such as identity were not detected, and orientation information was mostly confined to MEG signals — potentially contaminated by signal leakage. This raises a critical reconsideration of whether the prefrontal cortex truly broadcasts the entirety of conscious content or only abstract, categorical information, demanding a reevaluation of GNWT’s mechanistic claims.\n\nNotably, the study’s carefully selected paradigm focused on the contents of consciousness—examining variables such as category, identity, orientation, and stimulus duration—and moved away from traditional contrast paradigms that compare conscious versus unconscious conditions. By doing so, the research sidesteps confounds related to decision making or memory processes, offering a more precise test of the positive, specific predictions made by the two theories about the neural signatures of conscious content.\n\nThe collaboration’s methodological rigor shines through in its preregistered hypotheses, protocols, and analyses, which were agreed upon with the theory proponents prior to data collection and analysis. This approach guards against hindsight bias or selective reporting, enhancing the credibility and impact of the findings. It also sets a new standard for adversarial collaboration in neuroscience—where competing theoretical camps jointly specify testable predictions and submit them to stringent empirical scrutiny.\n\nDespite the comprehensiveness of the data, the authors acknowledge inherent limitations. Task engagement could not be entirely excluded, particularly concerning categorical processing, though mechanisms involving orientation and stimulus duration were designed to be task-irrelevant to mitigate such confounds. Moreover, while the multimodal imaging techniques provided complementary spatial and temporal resolutions, the absence of single-unit recordings, typically constrained to clinical populations with epilepsy, limits access to finer microcircuit activity that may be crucial to parsing consciousness.\n\nBeyond the direct challenges posed to IIT and GNWT, these findings ripple through the broader landscape of consciousness theories. For example, some higher-order theories that attribute the content of visual consciousness directly to prefrontal cortical processing similarly face reevaluation, given the observed inconsistencies in prefrontal representation. Conversely, local recurrency and recurrent processing theories, which emphasize posterior cortical mechanisms, partly share predictions challenged here, highlighting an imperative for theoretical refinement across the field.\n\nThe study also underscores an urgent need for formal frameworks to weigh theoretical predictions quantitatively and integrate diverse empirical findings. Currently, the team adopted a lenient falsificationist stance, considering evidence for any predicted feature sufficient to uphold a theory’s claim, rather than demanding consistency on all fronts. However, establishing computational or statistical models to balance prediction centrality, measurement noise, and cross-sample reproducibility will be indispensable for future theory development and adjudication.\n\nIn stark contrast to the often polarized discourse surrounding consciousness research, this adversarial collaboration champions openness and transparency. By jointly publishing results alongside adversaries’ interpretations, the consortium invites the scientific community to weigh evidence critically, acknowledging that theory evaluation isn’t a simple matter of acceptance or rejection but an ongoing, dynamic dialogue shaped by empirical data and cognitive biases alike.\n\nThis transformative research stands as a milestone, not only for consciousness science but for experimental philosophy of neuroscience. Its meticulous design, fine-grained methodology, and collaborative spirit demonstrate a powerful pathway toward converging on robust explanations of phenomenally rich human experience. The challenge now lies in integrating these insights to refine existing theories or perhaps forge new models that can withstand the rigorous tests of both data and philosophical scrutiny.\n\nAs the field advances, the integration of animal model studies, including invasive single-neuron recordings and causal manipulations, will complement human neuroimaging, filling current gaps and driving a truly comprehensive understanding of consciousness. Such multifaceted approaches, embracing adversarial collaboration and predicated on clear, testable theoretical predictions, might ultimately unravel one of science’s most profound enigmas: how subjective awareness arises from the brain’s neural fabric.\n\n**Subject of Research:**Testing and comparing Integrated Information Theory and Global Neuronal Workspace Theory of consciousness using multimodal brain imaging modalities.\n\n**Article Title:**Adversarial testing of global neuronal workspace and integrated information theories of consciousness.\n\n**Article References:**Cogitate Consortium., Ferrante, O., Gorska-Klimowska, U. et al. Adversarial testing of global neuronal workspace and integrated information theories of consciousness. Nature (2025). https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-025-08888-1\n\n**Image Credits:**AI Generated\n\nTags: adversarial collaboration in sciencechallenges to consciousness theoriesempirical evaluation in consciousness studiesexperimental design in neurosciencefunctional magnetic resonance imaging in researchGlobal Neuronal Workspace TheoryIntegrated Information Theoryintracranial EEG and consciousnessmagnetoencephalography applicationsmultimodal neuroimaging techniquesneuroscience of consciousnesstheory testing in neuroscience\n\n[**Share16](https://www.facebook.com/sharer.php?u=https%3A%2F%2Fbioengineer.org%2Fchallenging-global-workspace-and-integrated-information-theories%2F)[**Tweet10](https://twitter.com/intent/tweet?text=Challenging%20Global%20Workspace%20and%20Integrated%20Information%20Theories&url=https%3A%2F%2Fbioengineer.org%2Fchallenging-global-workspace-and-integrated-information-theories%2F)[**Share3](https://www.linkedin.com/shareArticle?url=https%3A%2F%2Fbioengineer.org%2Fchallenging-global-workspace-and-integrated-information-theories%2F&title=Challenging%20Global%20Workspace%20and%20Integrated%20Information%20Theories)[**Share](https://reddit.com/submit?url=https%3A%2F%2Fbioengineer.org%2Fchallenging-global-workspace-and-integrated-information-theories%2F&title=Challenging%20Global%20Workspace%20and%20Integrated%20Information%20Theories)[**Share](https://telegram.me/share/url?url=https%3A%2F%2Fbioengineer.org%2Fchallenging-global-workspace-and-integrated-information-theories%2F&text=Challenging%20Global%20Workspace%20and%20Integrated%20Information%20Theories)[**Share2](http://vk.com/share.php?url=https%3A%2F%2Fbioengineer.org%2Fchallenging-global-workspace-and-integrated-information-theories%2F)\n\n[**](https://www.linkedin.com/shareArticle?url=https%3A%2F%2Fbioengineer.org%2Fchallenging-global-workspace-and-integrated-information-theories%2F&title=Challenging%20Global%20Workspace%20and%20Integrated%20Information%20Theories)[**](https://www.pinterest.com/pin/create/bookmarklet/?pinFave=1&url=https%3A%2F%2Fbioengineer.org%2Fchallenging-global-workspace-and-integrated-information-theories%2F&media=https://bioengineer.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/Challenging-Global-Workspace-and-Integrated-Information-Theories.jpg&description=Challenging%20Global%20Workspace%20and%20Integrated%20Information%20Theories)[**](https://bioengineer.org/challenging-global-workspace-and-integrated-information-theories/)\n\n### Related**Posts**\n\n[\n\n](https://bioengineer.org/study-examines-depression-treatment-choices-among-veterans-health-administration-outpatients/)\n\n### [Study examines depression treatment choices among Veterans Health Administration outpatients](https://bioengineer.org/study-examines-depression-treatment-choices-among-veterans-health-administration-outpatients/)\n\n[**August 13, 2026](https://bioengineer.org/study-examines-depression-treatment-choices-among-veterans-health-administration-outpatients/)\n\n[\n\n](https://bioengineer.org/aat-serpina1-piz-variant-influences-gestation-aat-prevents-preterm-birth-in-mice/)\n\n### [AAT/SERPINA1 Pi*Z Variant Influences Gestation; AAT Prevents Preterm Birth in Mice](https://bioengineer.org/aat-serpina1-piz-variant-influences-gestation-aat-prevents-preterm-birth-in-mice/)\n\n[**August 13, 2026](https://bioengineer.org/aat-serpina1-piz-variant-influences-gestation-aat-prevents-preterm-birth-in-mice/)\n\n### [Drug-Resistant Rest Tremor Linked to Slower Parkinson’s Disease Progression](https://bioengineer.org/drug-resistant-rest-tremor-linked-to-slower-parkinsons-disease-progression/)\n\n[**August 13, 2026](https://bioengineer.org/drug-resistant-rest-tremor-linked-to-slower-parkinsons-disease-progression/)\n\n### [Turning AI Breakthroughs Into Public Health Action](https://bioengineer.org/turning-ai-breakthroughs-into-public-health-action/)\n\n[**August 13, 2026](https://bioengineer.org/turning-ai-breakthroughs-into-public-health-action/)\n\n### POPULAR NEWS\n- \n\n[\n\n](https://bioengineer.org/one-country-two-oceans-twenty-times-more-sharks/)\n\n### [One Country, Two Oceans, Twenty Times More Sharks](https://bioengineer.org/one-country-two-oceans-twenty-times-more-sharks/)\n\n**29 shares\n\n[Share12](https://www.facebook.com/sharer.php?u=https%3A%2F%2Fbioengineer.org%2Fone-country-two-oceans-twenty-times-more-sharks%2F)[Tweet7](https://twitter.com/intent/tweet?text=One%20Country%2C%20Two%20Oceans%2C%20Twenty%20Times%20More%20Sharks&url=https%3A%2F%2Fbioengineer.org%2Fone-country-two-oceans-twenty-times-more-sharks%2F)\n- \n\n### [Most smokers remain unaware that smoking dramatically increases heart disease risk](https://bioengineer.org/most-smokers-remain-unaware-that-smoking-dramatically-increases-heart-disease-risk/)\n\n**29 shares\n\n[Share12](https://www.facebook.com/sharer.php?u=https%3A%2F%2Fbioengineer.org%2Fmost-smokers-remain-unaware-that-smoking-dramatically-increases-heart-disease-risk%2F)[Tweet7](https://twitter.com/intent/tweet?text=Most%20smokers%20remain%20unaware%20that%20smoking%20dramatically%20increases%20heart%20disease%20risk&url=https%3A%2F%2Fbioengineer.org%2Fmost-smokers-remain-unaware-that-smoking-dramatically-increases-heart-disease-risk%2F)\n- \n\n### [Gluons Carry the Proton’s Baryon Number, Study Finds](https://bioengineer.org/gluons-carry-the-protons-baryon-number-study-finds/)\n\n**29 shares\n\n[Share12](https://www.facebook.com/sharer.php?u=https%3A%2F%2Fbioengineer.org%2Fgluons-carry-the-protons-baryon-number-study-finds%2F)[Tweet7](https://twitter.com/intent/tweet?text=Gluons%20Carry%20the%20Proton%E2%80%99s%20Baryon%20Number%2C%20Study%20Finds&url=https%3A%2F%2Fbioengineer.org%2Fgluons-carry-the-protons-baryon-number-study-finds%2F)\n- \n\n### [Study examines depression treatment choices among Veterans Health Administration outpatients](https://bioengineer.org/study-examines-depression-treatment-choices-among-veterans-health-administration-outpatients/)\n\n**29 shares\n\n[Share12](https://www.facebook.com/sharer.php?u=https%3A%2F%2Fbioengineer.org%2Fstudy-examines-depression-treatment-choices-among-veterans-health-administration-outpatients%2F)[Tweet7](https://twitter.com/intent/tweet?text=Study%20examines%20depression%20treatment%20choices%20among%20Veterans%20Health%20Administration%20outpatients&url=https%3A%2F%2Fbioengineer.org%2Fstudy-examines-depression-treatment-choices-among-veterans-health-administration-outpatients%2F)\n\n### About\n\nWe bring you the latest biotechnology news from best research centers and universities around the world. Check our website.\n\n### Follow us\n\n[**](https://www.facebook.com/Bioengineer.org/)[**](https://twitter.com/bioengineerorg)[**](https://www.instagram.com/bioengineering/)\n\n### Recent News\n\n### [One Country, Two Oceans, Twenty Times More Sharks](https://bioengineer.org/one-country-two-oceans-twenty-times-more-sharks/)\n\n### [Most smokers remain unaware that smoking dramatically increases heart disease risk](https://bioengineer.org/most-smokers-remain-unaware-that-smoking-dramatically-increases-heart-disease-risk/)\n\n### [Gluons Carry the Proton’s Baryon Number, Study Finds](https://bioengineer.org/gluons-carry-the-protons-baryon-number-study-finds/)\n\n### Subscribe to Blog via Email\n\nJoin 86 other subscribers\n\n- [Contact Us](https://bioengineer.org/contact-us/)\n\n[Bioengineer.org](https://bioengineer.org)© Copyright 2023 All Rights Reserved.\n\n[**](https://bioengineer.org/challenging-global-workspace-and-integrated-information-theories/#back-to-top)\n\n[**](https://bioengineer.org/challenging-global-workspace-and-integrated-information-theories/)\n\n[**](https://bioengineer.org/challenging-global-workspace-and-integrated-information-theories/)\n\nNo Result\n\n**View All Result\n\n- [Homepages](https://bioengineer.org/)\n- [Home Page 1](https://bioengineer.org/)\n- [Home Page 2](https://bioengineer.org/home-2/)\n- [News](https://bioengineer.org/category/news/)\n- [National](https://bioengineer.org/category/national/)\n- [Business](https://bioengineer.org/category/news/business/)\n- [Health](https://bioengineer.org/category/news/science-news/health/)\n- [Lifestyle](https://bioengineer.org/category/lifestyle/)\n- [Science](https://bioengineer.org/category/news/science-news/science/)\n\n[**](https://www.facebook.com/Bioengineer.org/)[**](https://twitter.com/bioengineerorg)[**](https://bioengineer.org/challenging-global-workspace-and-integrated-information-theories/)[**](https://bioengineer.org/challenging-global-workspace-and-integrated-information-theories/)\n\n[Bioengineer.org](https://bioengineer.org)© Copyright 2023 All Rights Reserved.", "error": null, "created_at": "2026-08-13T19:33:29.606877+00:00"}, {"id": "tool-receipt:18fc7b8cca3048e8831521d6", "attempt_id": "attempt:741dfef04e3323e6516ba599", "server": "anysearch", "tool": "extract", "arguments": {"url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/36763595/"}, "result": "## pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov\n\n**Source**: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/36763595/\n\n---\n\n# Cookies must be enabled\n\nEnable cookies forpubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.govand reload this page to continue.", "error": null, "created_at": "2026-08-13T19:33:35.396231+00:00"}, {"id": "tool-receipt:1b9b0c81527838fdf76330f3", "attempt_id": "attempt:741dfef04e3323e6516ba599", "server": "anysearch", "tool": "extract", "arguments": {"url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction"}, "result": "## Orchestrated objective reduction - Wikipedia\n\n**Source**: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction\n\n---\n\n[Jump to content](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#bodyContent)\n\nFrom Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia\n\nTheory of a quantum origin of consciousness\n\n[](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Roger_Penrose_9671.JPG)\n\n[](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Stuart_Hameroff_TASC2008.JPG)\n\nThe founders of the theory:[Roger Penrose](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roger_Penrose)(left) and[Stuart Hameroff](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stuart_Hameroff)\n\n**Orchestrated objective reduction**(**Orch OR**) is a controversial theory postulating that[consciousness](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Consciousness)originates at the[quantum level](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quantum_mind)inside[neurons](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Neurons)(rather than being a product of[neural connections](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Neural_pathway)). The mechanism is held to be a[quantum](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quantum_physics)process called[objective reduction](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Objective_reduction)that is orchestrated by cellular structures called[microtubules](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Microtubule). It is proposed that the theory may answer the[hard problem of consciousness](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hard_problem_of_consciousness)and provide a mechanism for[free will](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Free_will).[[1]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-frontiers-1)The hypothesis was put forward in the 1990s by physicist[Roger Penrose](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roger_Penrose)and[anesthesiologist](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anesthesiologist)[Stuart Hameroff](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stuart_Hameroff); it combines[molecular biology](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Molecular_biology),[neuroscience](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Neuroscience),[pharmacology](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pharmacology),[philosophy](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philosophy),[quantum information theory](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quantum_information_theory), and[quantum gravity](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quantum_gravity).[[2]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-H&PvsReimers2014-2)[[3]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-Penrose2014-3)\n\nWhile some other theories assert that consciousness emerges as the complexity of the[computations](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Computation)performed by[cerebral](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cerebral_cortex)[neurons](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Neuron)increases,[[4]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-McCulloch1943-4)[[5]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-Hodgkin1952-5)Orch OR posits that consciousness is based on[non-computable](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Computability_theory)[quantum processing](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quantum_computing)performed by[qubits](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Qubits)formed collectively on cellular microtubules, a process significantly amplified in the neurons. The qubits are based on oscillating[dipoles](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dipoles)forming[superposed](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quantum_superposition)resonance rings in helical pathways throughout lattices of microtubules. The oscillations are either electric, due to charge separation from[London forces](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/London_forces), or magnetic, due to[electron spin](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Electron_spin)—and possibly also due to[nuclear spins](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nuclear_spin)(that can remain isolated for longer periods) that occur in[gigahertz](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gigahertz),[megahertz](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Megahertz), and[kilohertz](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kilohertz)frequency ranges.[[2]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-H&PvsReimers2014-2)[[6]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-HameroffVs7Others2014-6)Orchestration refers to the hypothetical process by which connective proteins, such as[microtubule-associated proteins](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Microtubule-associated_protein), influence or orchestrate qubit[state reduction](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wave-function_collapse)by modifying the spacetime-separation of their superimposed states.[[7]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-Penrose-Hameroff2014-7)The latter is based on[Penrose's objective-collapse theory](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Penrose_interpretation)for interpreting quantum mechanics, which postulates the existence of an objective threshold governing the collapse of[quantum states](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quantum_state), related to the difference of the[spacetime curvature](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spacetime_curvature)of these states in the universe's[fine-scale](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Planck_scale)structure.[[8]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-8)\n\nOrchestrated objective reduction has been criticized from its inception by mathematicians, philosophers,[[9]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-Boolos_1990-9)[[10]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-martin_1993-10)[[11]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-lewis_1969-11)[[12]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-putnam_1995-12)[[13]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-13)and scientists.[[14]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-Tegmark2000-14)[[15]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-Koch2006-15)[[16]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-Hepp2012-16)These criticisms focus on three issues: Penrose's interpretation of[Gödel's theorem](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/G%C3%B6del%27s_incompleteness_theorems); Penrose's[abductive reasoning](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abductive_reasoning), linking non-computability to quantum events; and the brain's unsuitability to host the quantum phenomena required by the theory, since it is considered too \"warm, wet and noisy\" to avoid[decoherence](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Decoherence).\n\n## Background[[edit](https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Orchestrated_objective_reduction&action=edit&section=1)]\n\nFurther information:[Penrose–Lucas argument](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Penrose%E2%80%93Lucas_argument)\n\nIn 1931, mathematician and logician[Kurt Gödel](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kurt_G%C3%B6del)proved that any[effectively generated](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Effective_procedure)theory capable of proving basic arithmetic cannot be both[consistent](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Consistency)and[complete](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Completeness_(logic)). In other words, a mathematically sound theory lacks the means to prove itself.[[17]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-17)In his first book concerning consciousness,*[The Emperor's New Mind](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Emperor's_New_Mind)*(1989),[Roger Penrose](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roger_Penrose)argued that equivalent statements to \"Gödel-type propositions\" had recently been put forward.[[18]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-Penrose1989-18)\n\nPartially in response to Gödel's argument, the[Penrose–Lucas argument](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Penrose%E2%80%93Lucas_argument)leaves the question of the physical basis of non-[computable](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Computable_function)behavior open. Most physical laws are computable, and thus algorithmic. However, Penrose determined that[wave function collapse](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wave_function_collapse)was a prime candidate for a non-computable process. In[quantum mechanics](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quantum_mechanics), particles are treated differently from the objects of[classical mechanics](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Classical_mechanics). Particles are described by[wave functions](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wave_function)that evolve according to the[Schrödinger equation](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Schr%C3%B6dinger_equation). Non-stationary wave functions are[linear combinations](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Linear_combination)of the[eigenstates](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eigenstate)of the system, a phenomenon described by the[superposition principle](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Superposition_principle). When a quantum system interacts with a classical system—i.e., when an[observable](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Observable)is measured—the system appears to[collapse](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wave_function_collapse)to a random eigenstate of that observable from a classical vantage point.\n\nIf collapse is truly random, then no process or algorithm can deterministically predict its outcome. This provided Penrose with a candidate for the physical basis of the non-computable process that he hypothesized to exist in the brain. However, he disliked the random nature of environmentally induced collapse, as randomness was not a promising basis for mathematical understanding. Penrose proposed that isolated systems may still undergo a new form of wave function collapse, which he called objective reduction (OR).[[7]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-Penrose-Hameroff2014-7)\n\nPenrose sought to reconcile[general relativity](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/General_relativity)and quantum theory using his own ideas about the possible structure of[spacetime](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spacetime).[[18]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-Penrose1989-18)[*[pageneeded](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Citing_sources)*][[19]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-Penrose1994-19)He suggested that at the[Planck scale](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Planck_scale), curved spacetime is not continuous, but discrete. He further postulated that each separated[quantum superposition](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quantum_superposition)has its own piece of[spacetime curvature](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spacetime_curvature), a blister in spacetime. Penrose suggests that gravity exerts a force on these spacetime blisters, which become unstable above the Planck scale of10−35m{\\displaystyle 10^{-35}{\\text{m}}}and collapse to just one of the possible states. The rough threshold for OR is given by Penrose's indeterminacy principle:τ≈ℏ/EG{\\displaystyle \\tau \\approx \\hbar /E_{G}}where:\n- τ{\\displaystyle \\tau }is the time until OR occurs,\n- EG{\\displaystyle E_{G}}is the gravitational self-energy or the degree of spacetime separation given by the superpositioned mass, and\n- ℏ{\\displaystyle \\hbar }is the[reduced Planck constant](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reduced_Planck_constant).\n\nThus, the greater the mass–energy of the object, the faster it will undergo OR and vice versa.[Mesoscopic](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mesoscopic_physics)objects could collapse on a timescale relevant to neural processing.[[7]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-Penrose-Hameroff2014-7)[*[additional citation(s) needed](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Citation_needed)*]\n\nAn essential feature of Penrose's theory is that the choice of states when objective reduction occurs is selected neither randomly (as are choices following wave function collapse) nor algorithmically. Rather, states are selected by a \"non-computable\" influence embedded in the[Planck](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Planck)scale of spacetime geometry. Penrose claimed that such information is[Platonic](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Platonism), representing pure mathematical truths, which relates to Penrose's ideas concerning the three worlds: the physical, the mental, and the Platonic mathematical world. In*[Shadows of the Mind](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shadows_of_the_Mind)*(1994), Penrose briefly indicates that this Platonic world could also include aesthetic and ethical values, but he does not commit to this further hypothesis.[[19]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-Penrose1994-19)\n\nThe Penrose–Lucas argument has been criticized by mathematicians,[[20]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-laforte_1998-20)[[21]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-solomon_1996-21)[[22]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-krajewski_2007-22)computer scientists,[[12]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-putnam_1995-12)and philosophers,[[23]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-mindpapers-23)[[24]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-lucas_criticisms-24)[[9]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-Boolos_1990-9)[[10]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-martin_1993-10)[[11]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-lewis_1969-11)and the consensus among experts in these fields is that the argument fails,[[25]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-25)[[26]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-26)[[27]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-Burgess-27)with different authors attacking various aspects of it.[[27]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-Burgess-27)[[28]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-28)[Marvin Minsky](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marvin_Minsky)has argued that because humans can believe false ideas to be true, human mathematical understanding need not be consistent, and consciousness may easily have a deterministic basis.[[29]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-29)[Solomon Feferman](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Solomon_Feferman)has argued that mathematicians do not progress by mechanistic search through proofs, but by trial-and-error reasoning, insight, and inspiration, and that machines do not share this approach with humans.[[21]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-solomon_1996-21)\n\n## Orch OR[[edit](https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Orchestrated_objective_reduction&action=edit&section=2)]\n\nPenrose outlined a predecessor to Orch OR in*The Emperor's New Mind*, coming to the problem from a mathematical viewpoint and in particular Gödel's theorem, but it lacked a detailed proposal for how quantum processes could be implemented in the brain.[Stuart Hameroff](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stuart_Hameroff)separately worked in cancer research and[anesthesia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anesthesia), which gave him an interest in brain processes. Hameroff read Penrose's book and suggested to him that[microtubules](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Microtubule)within neurons were suitable candidate sites for quantum processing, and ultimately for consciousness.[[30]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-Hameroff1982-30)[[31]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-Hameroff1987-31)Throughout the 1990s, the two collaborated on the Orch OR theory, which Penrose published in*[Shadows of the Mind](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shadows_of_the_Mind)*(1994).[[19]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-Penrose1994-19)\n\nHameroff's contribution to the theory derived from his study of the neural[cytoskeleton](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cytoskeleton), and particularly on microtubules.[[31]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-Hameroff1987-31)As neuroscience has progressed, the role of the cytoskeleton and microtubules has assumed greater importance. In addition to providing structural support, microtubule functions include[axoplasmic transport](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Axoplasmic_transport)and control of the cell's movement, growth, and shape.[[31]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-Hameroff1987-31)\n\nOrch OR combines the Penrose–Lucas argument with Hameroff's hypothesis on quantum processing in microtubules. It proposes that when condensates in the brain undergo an objective wave function reduction, their collapse connects noncomputational decision-making to experiences embedded in spacetime's fundamental geometry. The theory further proposes that the microtubules both influence and are influenced by the conventional activity at the synapses between neurons.\n\n### Microtubule computation[[edit](https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Orchestrated_objective_reduction&action=edit&section=3)][](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Microtubule_diagram.jpg)**A:**An[axon terminal](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Axon_terminal)releases[neurotransmitters](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Neurotransmitter)through a synapse, which are received by microtubules in a neuron's[dendritic spine](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dendritic_spine).\n**B:**Simulated microtubule tubulins switch states.[[1]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-frontiers-1)\n\nHameroff proposed that microtubules were suitable candidates for quantum processing.[[31]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-Hameroff1987-31)Microtubules are made up of[tubulin](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tubulin)protein subunits. The tubulin protein[dimers](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dimer_(biochemistry))of the microtubules have[hydrophobic](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hydrophobic)pockets that may contain delocalized[π electrons](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/%CE%A0_electron). Tubulin has other, smaller non-polar regions, for example eight[tryptophans](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tryptophan)per tubulin, which contain π electron-rich[indole](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Indole)rings distributed throughout tubulin with separations of roughly 2nm. Hameroff claims that this is close enough for the tubulin π electrons to become[quantum entangled](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quantum_entanglement).[[32]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-Hameroff_2007-32)During entanglement, particle states become inseparably correlated.\n\nHameroff originally suggested in the fringe*[Journal of Cosmology](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Journal_of_Cosmology)*that the tubulin-subunit electrons would form a[Bose–Einstein condensate](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bose%E2%80%93Einstein_condensate).[[33]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-Penrose-Hameroff2011-33)He then proposed a[Frohlich condensate](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Frohlich_condensate), a hypothetical coherent oscillation of dipolar molecules. However, this too was rejected by Reimers's group.[[34]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-Reimers2009-34)Hameroff and Penrose contested the conclusion, noting that Reimers's microtubule model was oversimplified.[[35]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-35)\n\nHameroff then proposed that condensates in microtubules in one[neuron](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Neuron)can link with microtubule condensates in other neurons and[glial cells](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Glial_cell)via the[gap junctions](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gap_junctions)of[electrical synapses](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Electrical_synapse).[[36]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-Hameroff2006a-36)[[37]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-Hameroff2009-37)He proposed that the gap between the cells is sufficiently small that quantum objects can[tunnel](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quantum_tunneling)across it, allowing them to extend across a large area of the brain. He further postulated that the action of this large-scale quantum activity is the source of 40Hz[gamma waves](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gamma_wave), building upon the much less controversial theory that gap junctions are related to gamma oscillation.[[38]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-Bennett&Zukin2004-38)\n\n## Experimental results[[edit](https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Orchestrated_objective_reduction&action=edit&section=4)]\n\n### Superradiance[[edit](https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Orchestrated_objective_reduction&action=edit&section=5)]\n\nIn a study Hameroff was part of,[Jack Tuszyński](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jack_Tuszy%C5%84ski)of the[University of Alberta](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/University_of_Alberta)demonstrated that anesthetics hasten the duration of a process called delayed luminescence, in which microtubules and tubulinsre-emittrapped light. Tuszyński suspects that the phenomenon has a quantum origin, with[superradiance](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Superradiance)being investigated as one possibility (in a 2024 study, superradiance was confirmed to occur in networks of[tryptophans](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tryptophan), which are found in microtubules).[[39]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-:0-39)[[40]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-:1-40)Tuszyński told*New Scientist*that \"We're not at the level of interpreting this physiologically, saying 'Yeah, this is where consciousness begins,' but it may.\"[[41]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-41)\n\nThe 2024 study, called \"Ultraviolet Superradiance from Mega-Networks of Tryptophan in Biological Architectures\" and published in*[The Journal of Physical Chemistry](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Journal_of_Physical_Chemistry)*, confirmed superradiance in networks of tryptophans.[[39]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-:0-39)[[40]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-:1-40)Large networks of tryptophans are a warm and noisy environment, in which quantum effects typically are not expected to take place.[[39]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-:0-39)The results of the study were theoretically predicted and then experimentally confirmed by the researchers.[[39]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-:0-39)[[40]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-:1-40)[Majed Chergui](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Majed_Chergui), who led the experimental team, stated that \"It's a beautiful result. It took very precise and careful application of standard protein spectroscopy methods, but guided by the theoretical predictions of our collaborators, we were able to confirm a stunning signature of superradiance in a micron-scale biological system.\"[[39]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-:0-39)[Marlan Scully](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marlan_Scully), a physicist known for his work in the field of theoretical quantum optics, said, \"We will certainly be examining closely the implications for quantum effects in living systems for years to come.\"[[39]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-:0-39)The study states that \"by analyzing the coupling with the electromagnetic field of mega-networks of Trp present in these biologically relevant architectures, we find the emergence of collective quantum optical effects, namely, superradiant and subradiant eigenmodes.... our work demonstrates that collective and cooperative UV excitations in mega-networks of Trp support robust quantum states in protein aggregates, with observed consequences even under thermal equilibrium conditions.\"[[40]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-:1-40)\n\n### Microtubule quantum vibration theory of anesthetic action[[edit](https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Orchestrated_objective_reduction&action=edit&section=6)]\n\nIn an experiment,[Gregory D. Scholes](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gregory_D._Scholes)and Aarat Kalra of[Princeton University](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Princeton_University)used lasers to excite molecules within tubulins, causing a prolonged excitation to diffuse through microtubules farther than expected, which did not occur when repeated under anesthesia.[[42]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-42)However, diffusion results have to be interpreted carefully, since even classical diffusion can be very complex due to the wide range of length scales in the fluid-filled extracellular space.[[43]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-43)\n\nAt high concentrations (~5[MAC](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Minimum_alveolar_concentration)), the anesthetic gas[halothane](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Halothane)causes reversible depolymerization of microtubules.[[44]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-44)This cannot be the mechanism of anesthetic action, however, because human anesthesia is performed at 1[MAC](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Minimum_alveolar_concentration). (Neither Penrose or Hameroff claim that depolymerization is the mechanism of action for Orch OR.)[[45]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-45)[[46]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-46)At ~1 MAC halothane, reported minor changes in tubulin protein expression (~1.3-fold) in primary cortical neurons after exposure to halothane and isoflurane are not evidence that tubulin directly interacts with general anesthetics, but rather shows that the proteins controlling tubulin production are possible anesthetic targets.[[47]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-47)Further proteomic study reports 0.5 mM [14C]halothane binding to tubulin monomers alongside three dozens of other proteins.[[48]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-Pan2007-48)In addition, modulation of microtubule stability has been reported during[anthracene](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anthracene)general anesthesia of tadpoles.[[49]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-:3-49)The study, called \"Direct Modulation of Microtubule Stability Contributes to Anthracene General Anesthesia\" claims to provide \"strong evidence that destabilization of neuronal microtubules provides a path to achieving general anesthesia\".[[49]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-:3-49)\n\nComputer modeling of tubulin's atomic structure[[50]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-50)found that anesthetic gas molecules bind adjacent to amino acid aromatic rings of non-polar[π-electrons](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pi_electron)and that collective quantum dipole oscillations among all π-electron resonance rings in each tubulin showed a spectrum with a common mode peak at 613[T](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tera-)[Hz](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hz).[[51]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-Craddock2017-51)Simulated presence of eight different anesthetic gases abolished the 613 THz peak, whereas the presence of two different nonanesthetic gases did not affect the 613 THz peak, from which it was speculated that this 613 THz peak in microtubules could be related to consciousness and anesthetic action.[[51]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-Craddock2017-51)\n\nAnother study that Hameroff was a part of claims to show that \"anesthetic molecules can impair π-resonance energy transfer and exciton hopping in 'quantum channels' of tryptophan rings in tubulin, and thus account for selective action of anesthetics on consciousness and memory\".[[52]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-52)\n\nIn a study published in August 2024, an undergraduate group led by a[Wellesley College](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wellesley_College)professor found that rats given[epothiloneB](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Epothilone), a drug that binds to microtubules, took over a minute longer to fall unconscious when exposed to an anesthetic gas.[[53]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-53)\n\n## Criticism[[edit](https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Orchestrated_objective_reduction&action=edit&section=7)]\n\nOrch OR has been criticized both by physicists[[14]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-Tegmark2000-14)[[54]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-McKemmish2009-54)[[34]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-Reimers2009-34)[[55]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-Reimers2013-55)[[56]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-Villatoro2015-56)and[neuroscientists](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Neuroscientist),[[57]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-Baars2012-57)[[58]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-Georgiev2017-58)[[59]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-Litt2006-59)who consider it to be a poor model of brain[physiology](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Physiology). It has also been critiqued for lacking[explanatory power](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Explanatory_power): the philosopher[Patricia Churchland](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Patricia_Churchland)wrote, \"Pixie dust in the synapses is about as explanatorily powerful as quantum coherence in the microtubules.\"[[60]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-60)\n\n[David Chalmers](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/David_Chalmers)has argued against quantum consciousness, discussing instead how quantum mechanics may relate to[dualistic consciousness](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mind%E2%80%93body_dualism).[[61]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-Chalmers2003-61)He has expressed skepticism that any new physics can resolve the[hard problem of consciousness](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hard_problem_of_consciousness)[[62]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-Chalmers1995-62)[[63]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-Chalmers1997-63)[[64]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-David_Chalmers_1996-64)and argued that quantum theories of consciousness suffer from the same weakness as more conventional theories. Just as he has argued that there is no particular reason why specific macroscopic physical features in the brain should give rise to consciousness, he also holds that there is no particular reason why a specific quantum feature, such as the EM field in the brain, should give rise to consciousness.[[64]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-David_Chalmers_1996-64)\n\n### Decoherence in living organisms[[edit](https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Orchestrated_objective_reduction&action=edit&section=8)]\n\nIn 2000,[Max Tegmark](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Max_Tegmark)claimed that any quantum coherent system in the brain would undergo effective[wave function collapse](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wave_function_collapse)due to environmental interaction long before it could influence neural processes (the \"warm, wet and noisy\" argument, as it later came to be known).[[14]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-Tegmark2000-14)He determined the decoherence timescale of microtubule entanglement at brain temperatures to be on the order of[femtoseconds](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Femtosecond), far too brief for neural processing.[Christof Koch](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Christof_Koch)and[Klaus Hepp](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Klaus_Hepp)also agreed that[quantum coherence](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coherence_(physics)#Quantum_coherence)does not play, or does not need to play, any major role in[neurophysiology](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Neurophysiology).[[15]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-Koch2006-15)[[16]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-Hepp2012-16)Koch and Hepp concluded that \"The empirical demonstration of slowly decoherent and controllable quantum bits in neurons connected by electrical or chemical synapses, or the discovery of an efficient quantum algorithm for computations performed by the brain, would do much to bring these speculations from the 'far-out' to the mere 'very unlikely'\".[[15]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-Koch2006-15)\n\nIn response to Tegmark's claims, Hagan, Tuszynski, and Hameroff claimed that he did not address the Orch OR model but instead a model of his own construction. This involved superpositions of quanta separated by 24nm rather than the much smaller separations stipulated for Orch OR. As a result, Hameroff's group claimed a decoherence time seven orders of magnitude greater than Tegmark's, although still far below 25ms. Hameroff's group also suggested that the[Debye](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Debye_force)layer of[counterions](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Counterion)could screen thermal fluctuations, and that the surrounding[actin](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Actin)gel might enhance the ordering of water, further screening noise. They also suggested that incoherent metabolic energy could further order water, and finally that the configuration of the microtubule lattice might be suitable for[quantum error correction](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quantum_error_correction), a means of resisting quantum decoherence.[[65]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-Hagan2002-65)[[66]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-Hameroff2006b-66)\n\nIn 2009, Reimers et al. and McKemmish et al. published critical assessments. Earlier versions of the theory had required tubulin-electrons to form either[Bose–Einsteins](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bose%E2%80%93Einstein_condensate)or[Frohlich](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Herbert_Fr%C3%B6hlich)condensates, and the Reimers group noted the lack of empirical evidence that such could occur. Additionally, they calculated that microtubules could only support weak 8MHz coherence. McKemmish et al. argued that[aromatic molecules](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aromaticity)cannot switch states, because they are delocalized, and that changes in tubulin protein-conformation driven by[GTP](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guanosine_triphosphate)conversion would result in a prohibitive energy requirement.[[54]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-McKemmish2009-54)[[34]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-Reimers2009-34)[[55]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-Reimers2013-55)\n\nIn 2022, a group of Italian physicists conducted several experiments that failed to observe spontaneous radiation emissions predicted by the[Diósi–Penrose](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Di%C3%B3si%E2%80%93Penrose_model)collapse model, but that \"Penrose's original collapse model, unlike Diósi's, did not predict spontaneous radiation, so has not been ruled out.\"[[67]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-67)[[68]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-68)\n\n### The cellular iron storage protein ferritin quenches microtubule radiance[[edit](https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Orchestrated_objective_reduction&action=edit&section=9)]\n\nWhile some of the studies mentioned above purport to show superradiance and an influence of anesthetics on decreasing excitation diffusion through microtubules, those studies were performed under artificial conditions that failed to include proteins associated with microtubules like[ferritin](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ferritin),[[69]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-69)which quenches microtubule superradiance.[[70]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-70)Evidence published prior to those studies establishes that ferritin interacts with microtubules in vivo and is essential for microtubule stability and function.[[71]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-71)For instance, those studies overlooked that:\n- Studies of biophotons in the human body fail to find any evidence of[ultraviolet](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ultraviolet)(UV)[biophotons](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Biophoton).[[72]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-72)In contrast, at least one of the studies cited above that is relied on as evidence of microtubule superradiance in support of Orch-OR relies on earlier studies of UV biophotons measured in single-celled organisms like*E. coli*and respiratory deficient yeast as the basis for its contention that such biophotons are present in cells.[[73]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-73)[[74]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-74)That study also used[UV-vis](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/UV-vis)equipment with a light source that can generate 1020photons per second, which is not representative of neurons' environment.\n- Ferritin in the human body absorbs UV from external sources at least in the skin and in the cornea, where the levels of UV photons are much higher than measured biophoton levels of UV even in*E. coli*and yeast.[[75]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-75)[[76]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-76)Endogenous ferritin in neurons would absorb UV biophotons that might be emitted from chemical processes (at levels that are too low to measure), and those UV biophotons would not even reach microtubules to cause superradiance or energy transport.\n- Ferritin contains tryptophan residues, the same material in microtubules that is supposed to cause microtubule superradiance.[[77]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-77)According to one of the studies cited above, microtubule superradiance is based on special configurations of tryptophan residues. The failure of that study to consider additional ferritin tryptophan residues in the vicinity of microtubule tryptophan residues means that the study is not relevant to cellular environments that include ferritin (which is basically every cell). As noted above, ferritin perturbs tubulin in the vicinity of tryptophan residues, which invalidates an*a priori*assumption of that study.\n- Ferritin has stronger ionic interaction with microtubules than the anesthetics that were used in one of the studies cited above and has electrical and magnetic properties that those anesthetics lack.[[78]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-78)[[79]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-79)[[80]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-80)Even if anesthetics interact with microtubules, ferritin has stronger interactions with microtubules, which may explain why ferritin is able to quench microtubule fluorescence.\n\nIn summary, experiments trying to demonstrate microtubule superradiance involved unrealistic levels of UV light and artificial environments, and excluded cellular substances that would prevent microtubule superradiance and energy transport.\n\n### Neuroscience[[edit](https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Orchestrated_objective_reduction&action=edit&section=10)]\n\nFurther information:[Neuroscience](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Neuroscience)\n\nBiology-based criticisms have been offered, including a lack of explanation for the probabilistic release of[neurotransmitters](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Neurotransmitter)from presynaptic[axon terminals](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Axon_terminal)[[81]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-Beck1992-81)[[82]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-Beck1996-82)[[83]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-Beck1998-83)and an error in the calculated number of the tubulin dimers per cortical neuron.[[84]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-YuBaas1994-84)\n\nIn 2014, Penrose and Hameroff published responses to some criticisms and revisions to many of the theory's peripheral assumptions, while retaining the core hypothesis.[[2]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-H&PvsReimers2014-2)[[6]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_note-HameroffVs7Others2014-6)\n\n## See also[[edit](https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Orchestrated_objective_reduction&action=edit&section=11)]\n\n- [Copenhagen interpretation](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Copenhagen_interpretation)\n- [Electromagnetic theories of consciousness](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Electromagnetic_theories_of_consciousness)\n- [Holonomic brain theory](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Holonomic_brain_theory)\n- [Many-minds interpretation](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Many-minds_interpretation)\n- [Penrose interpretation](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Penrose_interpretation_of_Quantum_Theory)\n- *[Quantum Aspects of Life](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quantum_Aspects_of_Life)*2008 book\n- [Quantum mind](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quantum_mind)\n- [Quantum cognition](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quantum_cognition)\n\n## References[[edit](https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Orchestrated_objective_reduction&action=edit&section=12)]\n\n- [1](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_ref-frontiers_1-0)[2](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_ref-frontiers_1-1)Hameroff, Stuart (2012).[\"How quantum brain biology can rescue conscious free will\"](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC3470100).*Frontiers in Integrative Neuroscience*.**6**: 93.[doi](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Doi_(identifier)):[10.3389/fnint.2012.00093](https://doi.org/10.3389%2Ffnint.2012.00093).[PMC](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/PMC_(identifier))[3470100](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC3470100).[PMID](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/PMID_(identifier))[23091452](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23091452).\n- [1](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_ref-H&PvsReimers2014_2-0)[2](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_ref-H&PvsReimers2014_2-1)[3](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_ref-H&PvsReimers2014_2-2)Hameroff, Stuart; Penrose, Roger (2014). \"Reply to seven commentaries on \"Consciousness in the universe: Review of the 'Orch OR' theory\"\".*Physics of Life Reviews*.**11**(1):94–100.[Bibcode](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bibcode_(identifier)):[2014PhLRv..11...94H](https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/2014PhLRv..11...94H).[doi](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Doi_(identifier)):[10.1016/j.plrev.2013.11.013](https://doi.org/10.1016%2Fj.plrev.2013.11.013).\n- [↑](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orchestrated_objective_reduction#cite_ref-Penrose2014_3-0)Penrose, Roger (2014).[\"On the Gravitization of Quantum Mechanics 1: Quantum State Reduction\"](https://do